## Compatibility closure

- The final Phase 6 audit uses the pinned Java source and reconciles scoped
  client methods, overloads, inherited members, settings/read boundaries,
  inputs, errors, control flows, Rust crate/facade exports, Rustdoc, hidden
  items, Cargo features, environment settings, and Controller DTOs.
- Pinned `MQClientAPIImpl.java` inventory: 132 public declarations, 121
  distinct names, seven overloaded groups; all overload members are
  crosswalked and all 29 direct RPC invocation cases pass.
- `ConsumeMessagePopOrderlyService` operations are mapped to the existing
  `PushConsumer` lifecycle, orderly-lock owner, and POP workers. The pinned
  Java class has no public getter for its private owner field; no standalone
  Rust service type is claimed.
- `ThreadPoolMonitor`, `ThreadPoolWrapper`, and `ThreadPoolStatusMonitor` are
  pinned Java common utilities with no production references in
  `client/src/main` or `remoting/src/main`; their Proxy/server callers place
  them outside the audited Rust client scope.
- Remaining provider/runtime/diagnostic/JVM-identity differences are
  classified in `docs/compatibility/KNOWN_DIFFERENCES.md`; they are not
  unexplained client capabilities or configuration gaps. These include
  Tokio/Netty and Rust/JVM representation, user-accepted RustCrypto/rustls
  provider algorithm limits, diagnostic stack/exception/object-identity
  formatting, and deliberately constructed cross-process enum identity-hash
  collisions.
- A Phase 19 handoff inventory confirms all 17 validation IDs from Phases 0–17
  are registered among the 37 differential scenarios. This does not claim live
  Broker or Docker validation; those runs remain in the existing Phase 19
  scope.

